ers kind  of had a wall around initially just  because there was a lot of people coming  from the outside and and doing pieces  such as uh that the nightline piece uh  another local person came by and found  the one person hunting raccoons and and  put that on as a story so the  low-hanging fruit very much so yeah and  so um it took a tremendous amount of  time to build those relationships  especially with the mayor's office uh  what what you saw in the one scene was  it was basically the result of six  months of every single day checking in  and hopefully hoping that we would  actually get get access so  you know i think that  detroiters have seen the writing on the  wall for a very long time and i think  they were eager to tell the story but  they wanted to make sure that you know  we would  do an appropriate job you know with with  that there's this real insider outsider  us versus them mentality is a real chip  on the shoulder of detroiters um and and  there's also there's a lot more than  that there's a lot of affection for  their their city and so  they were a funny bunch in terms of  subjects nobody was dying to be filmed i  mean the best characters in documentary  we found don't really care if you're  there or not and they're the best  characters and documentaries are exactly  the same if the camera is rolling or not  the same there's the new creative class  that are just showing up and there's  lifelong detroiters and and those two  populations are are headed for a  collision i think there's really no  interaction between the two groups right  now um the people on the porch talk  about urban farming and drop the tomato  i mean they're talk you know the urban  farming is something talked about in the  new uh you know arrival you know that  community for the last two years and a  lot of detroiters have been out of that  conversation completely and that scene  just shows the disconnect that you know  something that's been widely talked  about and they had you 